WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.680
Alicia Frame: Thank
you all for coming.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.700
I'm Alicia Frame, and I'm
joined by Omkar More.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.850
And we are here to tell
you about fine-tuning

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.680
and reinforcement learning.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.920
I am the Product Lead
for Model Customization,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.710
Omkar is my engineering
counterpart,

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:17.670
and what we're going to tell you

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.150
about today is how you
can deploy, observe,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.290
and learn from your production
agents to get better, smarter,

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.870
cheaper, and faster models.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:28.860
One thing, just to call

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.220
out before I get started, is,
it's a little noisy in here.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.890
If you're having trouble
hearing, there are headsets

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.040
in the back that you can get
so that you can kind of cut

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.540
through the background noise,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.200
but I will try to be super
loud so you can hear me.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.140
And with that,
let's get started.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.780
So what are we going
to cover today?

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.960
We're going to start with the
basics, five minutes of slides,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.320
and we're going to talk
about what is fine-tuning,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.840
why fine-tune, and what
does this have to do

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.670
with production agents?

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.920
From there, we're going to
get started with the demos.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:02.970
The first demo we're going

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:06.810
to show you is how you can use
a technique called distillation

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:10.380
to create cheaper
and faster agents.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.330
Next, we're going
to deal with, what

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.340
if your teacher
isn't smart enough?

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.830
How can we use
reinforcement learning

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.410
to learn from our mistakes?

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.560
After that, one level
harder, we're going to talk

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.620
about interactive reinforcement
learning, and you're all going

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.910
to get a preview of our new,
low-level training API.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.130
Think of it as
PyTorch as a service.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.160
You get access to the
low-level training primitives,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.050
we manage the compute.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.270
we build better models for you.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.160
And then, after
you've had a little bit

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.370
of a panic attack of, "Wow,
that was a lot of content",

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.550
we're going to end with
coding agents for fine-tuning

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.640
and show you how you can
use our fine-tuning skill

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.510
to really use natural language
to describe your goals

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:58.080
and create better
models in minutes.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.110
And with that,
let's get started.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.560
So, before we dive
in, I want to situate you

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.210
in the bigger picture.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.640
We're part of the
Microsoft Foundry team,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.580
and Foundry gives you
your agent platform.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.010
The foundation is models.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.280
There are over 11,000
models in Foundry,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.100
and these models power
our agent runtime,

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.060
let you deploy and
host your agents,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.700
observe them, give them tools.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.980
And once you've got those
agents, we want them

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.720
to be better and smarter.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.210
So we offer tooling
to evaluate them,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.730
know if they're doing
what you want them to do,

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:38.630
and optimize them, improve
their performance based

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.760
on what you've observed.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.650
This talk is really
going to be focusing

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:46.430
on the Evaluate
and Optimize box.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.130
But I want to motivate this talk

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.000
and explain why we're here.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.820
We've been working on
fine-tuning for a while,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.580
and I feel like people are
finally starting to listen

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.270
to, "there is a reason
to customize models."

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:04.650
And what we're seeing now
is agents have taken off.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.360
Agents helped us make
this slide deck.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.980
Agents helped us make this demo.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.250
A third of enterprise software
apps say they're going

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.010
to embed agentic AI
within two years.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:14.790
I think that's wrong.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.490
I think the number is
higher and sooner.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:22.590
Half of teams already have some
kind of production agent today.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:28.260
The problem: agents consume 20
to 30 times more tokens per turn

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.820
than an old-fashioned
chat interaction.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:36.750
And so, all of a sudden, we have
way more models being deployed

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:41.190
for way more use cases,
consuming way more tokens.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.830
And all of a sudden,
we've broken the bank,

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:47.010
you've run out of tokens, your
credit card is maxed out.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.600
What do we do?

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.660
And so, if agents are
token-consuming machines,

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.860
fine-tuning lets you
create a reliable agent

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.380
without breaking the bank.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.540
And so, what does
fine-tuning do?

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.140
When you customize a model,
you can create a cheaper,

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.480
faster, smarter model.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.900
So, historically, I think when
people think about fine-tuning,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.180
they think about
improving model quality.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.910
Two years ago, a lot
of people said, "Wow,

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.210
I need to fine-tune
GPT-3.5 Turbo

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.730
to teach it about my domain."

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.340
Today, when we talk
about improving quality,

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.320
especially in the
context of agents,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.580
it's really about calling the
right tool at the right time

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.770
with the right inputs.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.140
Improving quality is
really about baking

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.320
in those instructions from
your prompt into the weights

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.320
of the model itself, and
that is super important.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.750
But it's not the full story.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.190
What we're seeing
more and more of --

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:47.420
and you saw this probably
in sessions where we talked

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.110
about Harvey or UiPath,

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:56.370
many companies are fine-tuning
smaller, cheaper, faster models

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:00.120
to power their production
agents for their use cases

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.030
with the same quality
as a frontier model

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.890
at a fraction of the price.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:10.200
So the targets of fine-tuning
are often 10 to 30 times cheaper

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.310
than a frontier model.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:17.400
So we're talking about
replacing GPT-5.4 with 4.1 nano

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.400
or a Qwen model,
something much cheaper.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.730
And so when you're looking at
something 30 times cheaper,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.580
even if it's consuming
30 times the tokens,

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.860
your budget still balances.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.060
The other thing we worry

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:33.750
about with agents is they're
chatty, but we're impatient.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:35.970
The big frontier
models can be slow.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.120
We've all sat there and
watched Claude churn,

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.170
and you're like, "I'm bored.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.022
I'm bored. Come on."

00:05:41.047 --> 00:05:43.890
Small models
stream tokens faster.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:47.850
So your agentic workflows, once
you've got that smaller model,

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:49.410
it responds more quickly.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:52.800
So you really now have kind
of the perfect solution.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.920
You've got a model that knows
what tools to call and how

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.800
to behave, and it's
cheaper, and it's faster.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.590
And so, because of this, we're
seeing a lot more traction

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.620
for fine-tuning
and customization.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:07.920
When we talk about optimization,
it's really a journey.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.830
So, in Foundry, you often
start from, "I built my agent.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:12.840
I've got to get
the prompt right.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:16.350
Can I give it instructions
to make it do what I want?"

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:17.760
Step two is, "Okay.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.210
I need to add context
management to my agent runtime.

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:24.210
I'm going to give it data,
grounding, and memory.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.850
And then, I can give it
tools so it can take actions

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.640
and call external things."

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.160
And then, at all this
time, I'm evaluating it.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:33.900
I'm checking if it's
doing what I want.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.780
I'm checking how
it's performing.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.590
Once we've got that, though, now
it becomes time to optimize.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.230
"Is my model doing
the wrong stuff?

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.120
Is it too expensive?

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.710
Is it too slow?"

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.270
And that's where
fine-tuning comes in.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.940
And so, this talk today
is really going to focus

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:55.680
on this right-hand side of
this optimization curve.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.920
And I already said, fine-tuning
is becoming more popular

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.160
as we see the rise of agents,
and we're seeing this in

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.600
the developer world.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.570
About half of developers
say that they want

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:10.740
to replace their out-of-the-box
models with fine-tuning.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.140
Three years ago, people
-- maybe four years ago,

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:16.140
people were like, "I'm going to
build my own foundation model."

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.220
And then, we kind of moved on,

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.720
and everyone said,
"Ah, you know what?

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.550
Out-of-the-box
models are amazing.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:22.440
I'm good. I don't
need to do this."

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.140
Now, people are starting to
look at their balance sheets

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.890
or their CSAT, and they're
saying, "Actually,

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:30.960
maybe fine-tuning is for me."

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.130
And this really gets back

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.400
to the cost compounding
in the agency loop.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.660
And it ends up being about
quality, latency, and cost,

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:43.410
all driving people
towards this decision.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.260
And now, I've said the word
"fine-tuning" like 30 times,

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.410
and some of you, probably anyone
who is not a data scientist,

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:51.720
is like, "What is fine-tuning?"

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:53.580
So I got to this a little late,

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.070
but fine-tuning is really
just your cheat code

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:57.960
to building a foundation model.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:01.860
So if we go back to, "Where
do these models come from?",

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:03.990
the way you build a
foundation model is you start

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:05.010
with pre-training.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:08.130
You have a massive amount
of unlabeled data,

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.980
and you start off by figuring
out next token prediction.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:12.090
So,"I've got one word;

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:14.340
what's the next
most likely word?"

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:17.640
Once you've got that, you
often use instruction tuning,

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:21.180
where you give prompt-response
data, task-specific data,

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.460
to teach the model
how to respond.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.790
And then, the final step is
going to be alignment tuning,

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.450
where you give the model human
preference data, reward models,

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:33.450
to really steer how you
want it to respond.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:35.040
You put these three
steps together,

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:37.200
you get your frontier model.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.320
Fine-tuning lets you kind
of skip the most expensive,

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.290
hardest, complicated part.

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:45.020
You pick up your generic,
off-the-shelf model that's

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.930
pretty good, and then
you get to choose,

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:50.210
"Do I want to do
supervised fine-tuning?",

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:53.270
so teach it to behave in a
certain way, give it data

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:55.470
to copy and learn from,

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:56.940
"Or do I want to align it?

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:58.110
Or do I want to do both?"

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.000
And so, you kind of
get a head start

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.000
in the customization
journey, add your data,

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.430
and create your own IP.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:08.130
And so, with that, we've kind
of given you the crash course

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.710
in fine-tuning, and we're going
to get to the first demo,

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.660
which is, I'm sure, what
you all care about.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:16.760
And so, point one is we're going
to do "cheaper and faster".

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:20.090
So a really common technique in
the world of model customization

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.460
and fine-tuning is distillation.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:27.650
And this is where you take a
large, smart model like GPT-5.5,

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:32.070
and you capture its traffic and
use that as your training data.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.650
What we're going to show
you is how you take traces

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.090
from your production agent,
filter and curate them,

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:41.720
create a training set, and from
that, generate your small,

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:44.870
fine-tuned model
that is just as good

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:47.880
as your high-quality
frontier model.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:51.090
So, with that, we're going
to switch to the demo.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.200
Omkar More: All right.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.840
So throughout this demo and
throughout the talk today,

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:57.270
we're going to see -- we're
going to show you a variety

00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:01.140
of techniques for post-training
with the intention of making all

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.590
of your models faster,
cheaper, and smarter.

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:09.720
So across all of these demos,
we're going to concentrate

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:12.060
on a single customer scenario.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:13.860
This customer scenario is

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:17.610
about building a
customer service agent

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:19.440
for a retail scenario.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.960
So let's say you
have a retailer,

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:26.400
and you have a customer service
agent that processes refunds.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:30.090
So a customer calls in, asks for
a refund for a particular item.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:31.410
The agent has to go ahead

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:34.320
and decide whether the
request was valid.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.630
It has to decide whether
the item could be returned.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:40.020
It also has to decide whether
the item should be replaced

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:41.130
or refunded.

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:44.750
So these are complex scenarios
that the agent has to navigate.

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:48.920
The agent can look at the
order details via tool.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:51.770
It could also go and look
at the fulfillment status.

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:54.500
It could go and look
at the policy details

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:56.300
and then process the payments.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:57.960
But what if it gets it wrong?

00:10:57.960 --> 00:10:59.460
If it gets it wrong,
it could lead

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:02.370
to the retailer losing
a lot of money.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.900
And as you know --

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.190
Alicia Frame: Or a
lot of customers.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:08.130
Omkar More: Or a lot of
customers, and online returns is

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:12.210
about $900 billion of
business in the US last year.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:15.210
So it's very critical for us
to get this scenario right.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.310
So with that setup,
let's go ahead and look

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:19.830
at how we can do distillation
from a larger model

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:23.730
like a 5.4 model to a smaller
4.1 or 4.1 mini model and see

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:27.150
if we can get the models to
be faster and cheaper, okay?

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:28.110
Alicia Frame: Okay.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:28.410
Omkar More: All right.

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:34.920
So I have, over here
-- let's go over to.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:36.060
All right.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:40.650
So let's go to our agents.

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:41.220
Okay.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:42.480
Alicia Frame: And so, as
you're pulling this up,

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.200
for folks who haven't used it
before, this is our Foundry UI.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.560
This is a really simple,
no-code way to build agents,

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:53.070
fine-tune models, but this is
also all possible from code.

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:54.000
Omkar More: Absolutely.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.390
So, to start with, I have
built a hosted agent.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:02.100
So Foundry allows you to use
any platform that you want,

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:05.580
any agent platform
like Semantic Kernel,

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:09.180
if you want to use LangGraph,
and host your agents on Foundry.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:10.740
So I have done that over here.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:15.660
This is an agent that is using
GPT-5.4 as its base model.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:19.060
And let's try it out
with an example.

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:25.930
Not this one.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.570
Maybe this one.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:31.320
All right.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:33.360
So the customer is asking
to return a yoga mat,

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:36.780
which they have not used, and
see if we can process a refund.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:40.260
Foundry agents allow --
Foundry-hosted agents allow you

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:43.410
to actually capture all the
traces so that you can use

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:47.400
that to evaluate and further
use it to fine-tune as well.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.800
So, as you can see, the
request has been resolved,

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.930
and you can look at the traces

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:56.010
to see what exactly happened
during the entire process.

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:57.390
Alicia Frame: So can
you show us the tools

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.010
that your agent is
calling in here?

00:12:59.010 --> 00:12:59.580
Omkar More: Absolutely.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:01.020
So here's what has happened.

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:05.040
So, if you see, the user view
shows you what the request was

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.570
and what the final outcome was.

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:09.030
But the trajectories
actually show you

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:10.380
which of our tools were invoked.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:13.470
In this case, the "get
order details" was invoked.

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.780
After that, we had the
"fulfillment status" invoked,

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.750
and then the "check
resolution policy",

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.670
and then the final
outcome was resulted.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:23.040
So this is really rich
data that you can use

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:26.790
to evaluate your models and
also use it to improve them.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.370
So, going back, let's go
ahead and look at the

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.550
"Traces" tab in hosted agents.

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.500
You'll see that this
agent has been working

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:36.840
for the last few days,
and we have about --

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:40.320
I think, about a thousand-odd
traces that have been captured.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:45.090
And we'll use these to actually
go ahead and evaluate the model.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:47.220
Now, the first step to
think about this is,

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:49.980
"Can I just replace my large
model with a smaller model

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:51.420
and see how it performs?"

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:53.940
So let's go ahead and build
up an evaluation scenario

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:56.040
and see how they stack

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:59.010
up in the first place before we
do any fine-tuning whatsoever.

00:13:59.010 --> 00:13:59.490
Alicia Frame: So, really,

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:02.280
you're basically showing
us that we're not lying.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.170
We're going to start
with no fine-tuning,

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:05.940
just smaller and cheaper.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:07.380
Let's see how well it works out.

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:07.740
Omkar More: All right.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:10.680
And this I'm going to
do with the SDK.

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:14.910
So let's go ahead
and start that up.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:22.560
So a few things to talk
about over here is --

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:25.300
let me just start
off this and then --

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.630
Alicia Frame: So are you
loading up all of your evals

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:33.660
to be able to check how
well the models perform?

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:34.680
Omkar More: Yes.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:38.220
So, now, Foundry allows you
to have different graders.

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:40.050
So it allows
out-of-the-box graders

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:43.980
like task resolution,
intent resolution.

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:47.490
So these are LLM-based graders
that can look at the trajectory

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:50.460
and tell what the intent was,
and was it actually done or not.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:51.930
So you could use that.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:54.630
Or you could get more control
by writing your own graders.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.360
And we allow you to write
graders backed by an LLM

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:59.880
or using pure
Python code itself.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:05.040
So, in this case, I have written
a Python grader, which allows me

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:08.370
to grade the responses
on three fronts:

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:11.580
whether the decision was right --
in case the refund was supposed

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:13.440
to be done or was it rejected.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:17.250
So I give 50% weightage to that.

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.520
How accurate was it,
from the dollar amount.

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:24.090
So if it was a $50 item,
was I refunding $500?

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:25.410
So that kind of thing.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:27.720
And then it also grades
about 20% weight

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.730
for the format itself,
because this is important,

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:32.580
because your downstreams are
dependent upon how the model

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:35.310
format is given.

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:37.350
Alicia Frame: And really,
without this, we have no way

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:40.890
of knowing, one, if the
agent works or not, but two,

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:43.560
whether fine-tuning makes
anything better or worse.

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:46.350
So this is really the
most foundational step.

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:47.490
Omkar More: Yes.

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:50.100
And then, I build an
evaluation, which has all

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:54.630
of these three graders and
then submit an eval run

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:56.760
for the base models.

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:58.110
And let's go ahead
and have a look

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:00.730
at the evaluations themselves.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:02.370
Alicia Frame: And so,

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:05.040
whenever you're generating
something from the SDK,

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:07.230
it's automatically
registered in the UI.

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:09.780
So if you want a beautiful
view of your eval,

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:11.400
you can just click
over here and see it.

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:12.270
Omkar More: Yeah.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.060
So, as you can see, the o4-mini

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:19.380
and GPT-5.4 do reasonably
okay, 65% quality score.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:21.930
But the smaller models,
which is 4.1-mini

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:24.480
and nano, perform really poorly.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:27.480
And you don't want to
replace them as-is just

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:29.100
to save cost and latency.

00:16:29.100 --> 00:16:30.540
So, what do we do?

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:31.800
Alicia Frame: Cry?

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.360
Omkar More: Well,
Foundry to the rescue.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.870
You can actually use the traces
that we have just captured

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:39.600
to fine-tune a smaller model
and see how it performs now.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:42.630
And they make it so simple
that you can actually do it

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:44.640
through the UI directly.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:48.090
So let me go ahead and
show you just that.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:49.380
So go to "Retail."

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:51.180
This is my agent.

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:53.820
We'll go back to the traces
that we saw earlier.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:58.290
And then, click on the
"Create Dataset" button.

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:02.910
This allows you to convert
your traces from raw traces

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:05.160
into datasets that you
can use for evaluation,

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:08.310
for reinforcement fine-tuning,
or for supervised fine-tuning.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.440
So it does a little bit
smarter things here.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:12.300
It removes all the duplicates.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.630
Your trajectories might have
non-interesting conversations

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.580
that can be taken off.

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:19.530
It will redact some of
the PII information

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:22.890
as well before it
uses it for training.

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:23.970
So I'm going to go and try

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:26.580
to do supervised
fine-tuning over here.

00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:32.790
You will see that we
have -- let me see.

00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:34.210
Okay. Hold on.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:41.430
All right.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:45.770
So, as you can see, I have
about 1,400 traces here.

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:48.140
Supervised fine-tuning -- and
we suggest you at least have

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:50.210
about a thousand samples.

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:52.010
When you go to "Reinforcement
Fine-Tuning," you will see

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:55.440
that we can do a lot more with
the smaller dataset as well.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:58.500
So I'm going to use a thousand
samples for here and then click

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:03.810
on "Start Fine-Tuning" to
start the wizard right here.

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:09.350
Let's go ahead and select
maybe GPT-4.1 mini or nano.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:11.750
You'll see a few options

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.300
that you can select while you
kick off a SFT run as well.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:17.550
So, for instance, the training
type gives you different SKUs

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:21.960
that you can use to govern
data residency and your cost.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.180
My favorite over here is
the developer preview,

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:26.430
which allows you to do
training at half of the cost

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:27.780
of a standard-tier job.

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:28.470
Alicia Frame: So
if you're worried

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:30.570
about fine-tuning
being expensive,

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.460
developer tier, 50% off.

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:33.360
Omkar More: Yes.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:34.830
It runs on our low-priority VMs,

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:35.940
so it could take
a little longer,

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:38.460
but for developers,
