00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:41.250
We also introduced at Build
today the data zone SKU

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:45.270
that allows you to have
residency guarantees in US.

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:48.360
So I'm going to use
just the standard for now.

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:51.090
Also, you'll see that the
datasets are already preloaded

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:53.370
from the -- converted from
the traces into here,

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:56.370
so you don't need to go
ahead and add any datasets.

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:58.710
Then, you can click on over here

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:00.270
to have it
automatically deployed

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:02.190
to a model after job completion.

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:03.630
We'll skip that for now.

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:05.700
And you'll see that we
have a few hyperparameters

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:07.020
that you can tweak.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:08.430
And for higher and more control,

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:09.870
you might need more
hyperparameters.

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:13.140
We'll show in the next few demos
how you can get more control

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:14.760
onto your fine-tuning process.

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:16.470
Alicia Frame: But here is
really just easy mode, right?

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:16.980
Omkar More: Yes.

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.870
So you can just go ahead and
click just a few clicks,

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:22.410
and then your training is started.

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:25.690
Let's go ahead and open up a
run that is already completed.

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:30.330
So this is a distillation
run that we did earlier,

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:35.370
and you can go ahead on the
"Monitor" tab to see the scores.

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.340
You'll see there that
the loss is reducing,

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:41.220
accuracy is increasing,
everything looking good.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:43.980
You can see that we --
not just the final model,

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:45.540
you'll also get checkpoints

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:48.600
for interesting cases wherein
the reward might have

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:50.460
increased before the last step.

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:53.160
You can use that to deploy
and have a model as well.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:56.010
Or if you think that you need
to further fine-tune this

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:58.590
with more data, you can
use continuous fine-tuning

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:01.200
and go ahead and
submit another run.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:03.840
In this case, the run
looks pretty good.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:06.330
I have actually gone ahead
and deployed this earlier.

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:08.910
I have created an agent on top
of it and ran the evaluation,

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:10.320
just in the interest of time.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:14.330
And we can go ahead and look at
that evaluation run right now

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:16.380
to see how it stacks up.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:21.680
All right.

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:26.150
And let's go ahead and
sort it by retail order.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:29.010
Okay. So, as you can see,
we have a new winner.

00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:33.600
So GPT-4.1 mini fine-tuned
actually outperforms o4-mini

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:36.240
and 5.4 as well,
which is amazing.

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:37.680
Now, you have --
your agents can --

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:38.460
Alicia Frame:
Fraction of the cost.

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:39.810
Omkar More: Fraction
of the cost.

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:42.480
Your agents are
faster and cheaper.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:43.860
Alicia Frame: And I guess
maybe a little smarter.

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:44.520
Omkar More: And slightly
more performant as well.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:45.810
Alicia Frame: And the
other thing I want to call

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:48.660
out is you did include those
default graders, right?

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:48.930
Omkar More: Yes.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:48.970
Absolutely.

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:51.060
Alicia Frame: The intent
resolution, task completion.

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:53.940
And what we can see there is,
we're not trying to fine-tune

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:57.660
to improve those, but our
fine-tuning hasn't eroded the

00:20:57.660 --> 00:20:59.010
base model's capabilities.

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:03.030
So one fear with fine-tuning is,
maybe I made it better at this,

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:04.410
but worse at other stuff.

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:06.750
But we have those
guardrails in place

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:09.270
to make sure that didn't happen.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:10.560
So that was awesome.

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:11.310
Omkar More: Thank you.

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:14.190
Alicia Frame: So what is
our current leaderboard?

00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:15.810
Seventy-four percent.

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:17.280
That's an improvement.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:18.900
We've brought down our budget.

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:22.560
We're still kind of holding with
that frontier model standard.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:27.210
But $900 billion, 74% of
that, we're still losing

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:30.300
over $100 billion with
spurious refunds.

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:33.750
So what do we need to do?

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:35.790
Sometimes your teacher
isn't smart enough.

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:38.640
So with distillation,
what we just showed you,

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:42.060
where you have your teacher
model, frontier class,

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:43.860
generate a bunch of data,

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:47.310
fine-tune a small model using
supervised fine-tuning.

00:21:47.310 --> 00:21:50.490
With those techniques,
super easy to do,

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:54.360
but you're really never going to
get smarter than your teacher.

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:57.720
The source of the training data,
the mark you're trying to hit,

00:21:57.720 --> 00:21:59.490
is the teacher performance.

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:01.890
But for us, if we
look at this and say,

00:22:01.890 --> 00:22:05.430
"74% is really not good
enough for our business;

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:07.140
we need a new technique."

00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:10.170
And that's where reinforcement
learning comes in.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:11.820
And so, in this scenario,

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:14.850
what you're doing is
the model itself learns

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:16.800
from its own mistakes.

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:19.500
So we define a grader, and
so all the work we put

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:21.210
into evaluating the baseline,

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:24.630
evaluating our fine-tuned
models, comes in handy.

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:29.460
We use that same grader to
score the model's own responses

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:31.650
and learn from those scores.

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:36.420
So your model generates sample
results, you score them,

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:39.390
you learn from the
best-performing results,

00:22:39.390 --> 00:22:43.680
and the model, over time, learns
how to reason, in this case,

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:45.930
through multiple different
tool calls that it has

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:47.790
to chain and put together.

00:22:47.790 --> 00:22:51.150
The one caveat, though, for
reinforcement learning is, most

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:54.870
of the time, it needs to
be a verifiable task.

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:58.260
So what you're doing is you're
grading if the response is good

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:00.750
or not and learning
from that signal.

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:03.720
If you can't grade it,
you can't learn from it.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.090
But the good news is
refunds are gradable.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:07.650
You can look at
it and say, "Yes,

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:10.800
you should have refunded that,"
or, "No, that was terrible."

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:12.690
And so, this is a
perfect scenario

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:16.170
where we can score whether
it's getting better or worse.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:19.770
So, next demo,
reinforcement learning,

00:23:19.770 --> 00:23:22.830
learning from the
agent's mistakes.

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:25.440
And so, before we dive
into the tech again,

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:28.020
I'm going to just spend 30
seconds explaining kind

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:30.150
of what's happening
under the hood.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:32.700
So, with reinforcement
learning, you send your prompt

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:36.390
to the model, and the model
generates multiple samples.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:37.950
These are called rollouts.

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:40.020
And so, here, the model
has generated three

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:41.610
possible samples.

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:45.360
The grader you defined
scores those samples.

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.730
And the quality of your
grader is basically going

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:50.340
to determine whether
this works or not.

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:52.620
If there's no signal, if
your grader is just like,

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:55.320
"Everything is wrong", or
"Everything is right",

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:57.000
there's nowhere to go.

00:23:57.000 --> 00:24:00.900
So you need a grader that, one,
grades the outcome you want,

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:03.210
and two, leaves room
for improvement.

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:05.850
So you need an initial
signal, and then you need

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:07.380
to iterate from there.

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:10.350
Once you've got your quality
score from your grader,

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:12.840
that information is
fed into your trainer.

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:16.890
You reinforce the correct
answers, you learn from them,

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:20.250
and the model learns to
reason about a new domain.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:23.070
And if that sounds a little
complicated, Omkar is going

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:25.410
to show you how simple
it is to do in our UI.

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:26.430
Omkar More: Thank you, Alicia.

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:31.050
So let's go ahead and look at
reinforcement fine-tuning.

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:34.860
So we saw in the previous
demo, we took a teacher model.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:37.860
The teacher model had the entire
trajectories spelled out.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:39.930
So what tools were
supposed to be called,

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:41.010
what were the responses?

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:43.650
All of this was spelled out, and
that was the training dataset

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:45.240
that was used for fine-tuning.

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:47.940
Supervised fine-tuning is
like learning by seeing

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:52.170
or copying what a
smarter person is doing.

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:53.580
But in reinforcement
fine-tuning,

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:57.120
what we do is we don't give
the exact trajectories

00:24:57.120 --> 00:24:59.610
or prescription of "These are the
tools that need to be called."

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:03.780
Instead, we give the ability
for the training process itself

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:07.260
to invoke these tools on the
fly and then take the responses

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:13.290
of those tools and use that
to grade against the grader

00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:15.660
that we wrote in the
evaluation flow,

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:18.510
and see which of these responses
are giving better results,

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:21.750
and then continuously try
to find out or reason about it

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:23.160
to make better decisions.

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:24.930
Alicia Frame: So you're
giving the models the tools

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:28.470
to answer the questions, and the
model figures it out on its own.

00:25:28.470 --> 00:25:29.340
Omkar More: Exactly.

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:32.880
So now let's go ahead and see
how you can do this in Foundry.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:34.800
So I'm here with the SDK.

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:37.380
So let's go step-by-step.

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:40.620
So we're going to have
the configuration done.

00:25:40.620 --> 00:25:44.490
And one thing that you want
to note over here is, look

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.830
at the training and
the validation data.

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:49.230
This data is, again, coming
from the traces itself.

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:52.200
But the training data right now
doesn't have the entire tool

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:52.830
call history.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:58.890
It only has the actual message
that the user had asked.

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:01.800
And then, it has some ground
truth that will be used

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:03.900
by the grader to grade this.

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:07.470
So for instance,
you will have the final score

00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:10.020
that has to be refunded, but
it won't have which tools

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.610
to be called to
get to that thing.

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:14.970
It wouldn't have the message
body itself that needs

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:17.430
to be returned, and so on.

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:22.290
So we do this, we upload it to
Foundry, and then let's go ahead

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:27.780
and look at the most important
part, which is the RFT grader.

00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:29.940
So you can use the
evaluation grader

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:32.670
that we already wrote itself,
or you could tweak it as well.

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:36.810
So, in this case, I have added
some coverage to tools itself,

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:38.400
which exact tools
are getting called.

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:41.220
So, for instance, I don't
want to have a high score

00:26:41.220 --> 00:26:45.570
for a response where the "get
orders" is not even invoked.

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:48.150
So, in those cases,
I can have control

00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:51.540
over how I want the
grader to behave.

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:52.830
Alicia Frame: And this
is really important

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:54.870
for avoiding things
like reward hacking.

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:56.670
So a failure mode
when we were working

00:26:56.670 --> 00:26:59.730
on putting this together was,
the model would get things wrong

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:02.520
and just learn, "I will
never call a tool",

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:04.770
because it was getting penalized
for calling the wrong tool.

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:05.820
Omkar More: Yes, and
you'll see that.

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:06.000
Yeah.

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.940
And when we'll see
the run itself,

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:11.790
you will see how you can avoid
this by looking at our telemetry

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:13.440
and the metrics
that will tell you

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:15.900
when such a thing does happen.

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:16.170
All right.

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:19.680
So now, we said we need to
give access to the tools

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:21.000
to the training run itself.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:22.020
How do you do that?

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:26.190
So, in this case, I have
hosted all of these tools

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:29.880
on a function app, and these
function apps can be invoked.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:32.310
These are real tool
calls that can be made.

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:33.750
It has some of this information.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:38.100
It's like your RL harness or
RL environment, so to say.

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:39.960
You could also use MCP servers

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:42.960
or anywhere else you
have your tools hosted.

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:47.320
And let's go ahead
and kick off a run.

00:27:50.850 --> 00:27:53.160
And let's go ahead and look
at this run through Foundry

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:54.840
to see what were
the hyperparameters

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:57.730
and how an earlier
run has performed.

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:08.880
Okay. This is queued.

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.910
So here's a completed run.

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:13.170
So you get a few
more things here.

00:28:13.170 --> 00:28:16.950
So you get access to a
few more hyperparameters

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:18.840
that are specific to
reinforcement fine-tuning,

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:21.180
especially to a reasoning
model like o4-mini.

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:24.150
You get to choose what is the
reasoning effort the model has

00:28:24.150 --> 00:28:24.540
to put in.

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:26.730
You can set it to
low, medium, or high.

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:29.730
This tells you how much chain
of thought the model has to do

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:31.650
so that it can come
to the right answer.

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:32.970
And this has implications

00:28:32.970 --> 00:28:36.840
on what your token cost
is going to be as well.

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:39.780
Next up, you can also change
the number of epochs,

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:42.510
evaluation interval,
and things thereabout.

00:28:42.510 --> 00:28:45.570
You will see this is how I
have configured the tools

00:28:45.570 --> 00:28:48.660
to be called to
the function app.

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:50.610
Alicia Frame: And so this
really tells the training run,

00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:52.110
"These are the
tools you can use."

00:28:52.110 --> 00:28:52.410
Omkar More: Yes.

00:28:52.410 --> 00:28:53.430
This is the real training run.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:56.160
When the training is happening,
the tools are getting invoked,

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:58.890
and then those results are
what is getting used rather

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:02.760
than just copying like
in supervised fine-tuning.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:04.260
Let's go ahead and look
at the "Monitor" tab

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:07.890
to see what the
results look like.

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:12.660
So the primary metric that you
want to look at is rewards.

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:14.610
Are the rewards
increasing over time,

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:15.750
and are the rewards increasing

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:17.880
for your validation
set over time?

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.580
As you can see, that looks good.

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:23.700
Both the train and validation
rewards are increasing.

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:24.630
The other interesting thing

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:28.860
to notice here is the
reasoning token mean per step.

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:31.800
You can see that the model
was trying to spend a lot

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.200
of reasoning effort early
on in the training process,

00:29:34.200 --> 00:29:37.320
but as it goes on, the
reasoning effort comes down.

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:39.000
So this, again, has
implications in terms

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:43.110
of how much tokens will be
used by your fine-tuned model,

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:45.960
because now the final checkpoint
will use a lot less reasoning

00:29:45.960 --> 00:29:47.880
than the base model itself.

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:48.780
Alicia Frame: And so, early on,

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:50.880
it's just calling a whole
bunch of different tools,

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:52.890
trying to figure it out.

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:54.000
Omkar More: And the other
thing you would look

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.070
at is the number of
tool calls per rollout.

00:29:56.070 --> 00:29:59.130
So, in case it determined
that, "Hey, I don't want to --"

00:29:59.130 --> 00:30:00.510
or penalized tool calling itself

00:30:00.510 --> 00:30:02.520
and then stop calling
tools altogether,

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:04.740
you will notice a dip over
here and then say that, "Hey,

00:30:04.740 --> 00:30:07.380
there's something wrong with
this run," and you would want

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:11.910
to iterate on top of your
grader to tweak these things.

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:15.120
Okay. So, with that done,
let's go ahead and look

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:18.330
at the evaluations on how
this model fares amongst

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:19.980
our leaderboard.

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:21.480
So we have done
the distillation.

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:23.520
We have looked at the RFT evals.

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:26.140
Let's go and check it out.

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:31.800
So, again, we have a new winner.

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:35.190
We have o4-mini
coming up at 84%.

00:30:35.190 --> 00:30:41.250
Yeah. So that's a huge bump up
from where it was at 75 -- 73%.

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:42.270
And the intent resolution

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:43.980
and task completion
are also increasing

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:46.770
or maybe staying stable.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:50.460
So this is how you can use --
the more effort that you put in,

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:51.360
the better what you get.

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:54.420
So, for instance, reinforcement
learning can be a bit

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:56.010
of an effort to get it right,

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:58.770
making sure that your
model graders are correct,

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:00.270
the runs can take
a little longer,

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:02.760
but the outcome is
also substantial.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:03.690
Alicia Frame: And importantly,

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:07.020
we're still keeping true to that
better, cheaper, faster, right?

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:09.180
Because o4-mini is
still a smaller model

00:31:09.180 --> 00:31:10.950
than 5.4, which is our teacher.

00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:13.200
Omkar More: Yeah, it's
substantially cheaper than 5.4.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.150
Alicia Frame: So going back
