00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:03.180
I want full control over
the hyperparameters."

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:05.580
And if that is you, get excited.

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:06.990
We're going to give
you a sneak peek

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:10.470
of our new,
interactive training API.

00:33:10.470 --> 00:33:12.810
Before we show you what it
is, it's helpful to just kind

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:17.130
of compare and contrast how the
two different services work.

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:18.660
So everything we've shown you

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:21.840
so far is, really,
managed fine-tuning.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:23.040
You are the user.

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:24.570
You pick your model.

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:27.270
You pick your
technique: SFT, RFT.

00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:30.690
You provide your data, and
you submit it to the service.

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:34.590
And the service does the
rollouts, grades the responses,

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:37.320
does the loss computation,
updates the weights,

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:40.470
and iterates as a closed
loop before it returns

00:33:40.470 --> 00:33:41.580
a checkpoint.

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:43.590
You can't mess with
what's happening

00:33:43.590 --> 00:33:45.870
in that service boundary.

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:50.040
Our new training API actually
gives you much more control.

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:54.450
You, as the practitioner, set
up the config, the recipe.

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:56.100
You invoke the rollout engine.

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:57.270
You compute the loss.

00:33:57.270 --> 00:33:58.680
You update the weights.

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:01.710
And then look, we're back
outside that service boundary,

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:04.200
so you update the grader itself.

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:06.840
And the big difference here
is you're controlling the

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:10.820
algorithm, the training
primitives, every aspect

00:34:10.820 --> 00:34:15.260
of the fine-tuning job, except
the GPU infrastructure.

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:17.720
So if anyone here has used
Pearl or attempted

00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:19.790
to use Pearl and failed,

00:34:19.790 --> 00:34:23.180
we're abstracting away all
the pain of figuring out,

00:34:23.180 --> 00:34:25.100
"How do I set up
these GPU clusters?

00:34:25.100 --> 00:34:26.840
How do I orchestrate this?

00:34:26.840 --> 00:34:27.770
Is this synchronous?

00:34:27.770 --> 00:34:28.760
Is it asynchronous?

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:31.140
Is it aggregated
or disaggregated?"

00:34:31.140 --> 00:34:32.640
We manage that for you.

00:34:32.640 --> 00:34:36.390
You focus on the algorithm, the
problem you're trying to solve,

00:34:36.390 --> 00:34:38.520
and we manage the compute.

00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:40.770
And with that, I'm going
to pass it off to Omkar

00:34:40.770 --> 00:34:42.540
to show you what this
actually looks like.

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:43.110
Omkar More: Thank you.

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:45.420
So, in the previous
first two demos,

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:48.480
we looked at the high-level
APIs that Foundry provides.

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.850
These are great for
most of the cases

00:34:50.850 --> 00:34:53.040
and for most of the people.

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:55.620
They are scenarios where you
just do fire and forget.

00:34:55.620 --> 00:34:57.570
You give the tools,

00:34:57.570 --> 00:34:59.700
you give the configuration,
you give the dataset,

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:01.830
and then let Foundry do its job.

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.770
But as I said, the more
effort you put in,

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:06.080
the more value you get.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:08.660
So if you put in a little
bit more effort and want

00:35:08.660 --> 00:35:10.970
to do it yourself,
we allow you to do

00:35:10.970 --> 00:35:15.180
that with these low-level APIs,
wherein you write your own code,

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:18.210
and you can use these
smaller primitives

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:21.450
for doing forward pass, backward
pass, optim stage, sampling.

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:26.510
Things that are inherent that
require GPUs, leave that to us.

00:35:26.510 --> 00:35:28.310
And you can write
your own recipes

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:30.800
that will give you
a lot more control.

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:34.730
So, in this demo, I'm going
to use our fine-tuning --

00:35:34.730 --> 00:35:39.540
fine-grade APIs to fine-tune
OSS model Qwen3 32B

00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:42.450
and see how it performs and
stacks with our scenario.

00:35:42.450 --> 00:35:43.230
All right?

00:35:43.230 --> 00:35:44.520
So let's get started.

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:48.630
But before that, just to
give you a visualization,

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:50.520
what does a recipe
really look like?

00:35:50.520 --> 00:35:55.620
A recipe has specific
steps, like, "Hey,

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:57.420
first you're going
to take the dataset.

00:35:57.420 --> 00:35:59.580
You're going to generate
multiple samples out of it."

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:02.310
So, essentially, let's say
one conversation request.

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:05.280
You could have 10 or
20 samples generated.

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:09.930
That's our different options
that the model is emitting out.

00:36:09.930 --> 00:36:13.980
For that, you're going
to call our sample API,

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:19.920
which runs on GPUs where we have
preloaded a base LLM model,

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:21.330
and give you the responses.

00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:23.220
You take these samples
and then go ahead

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:25.080
and write your own grader.

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:26.280
You can invoke your own grader

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:28.740
to see how these
samples were behaving.

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:30.540
And earlier, the
grader was supposed

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:32.580
to be limited to
what we support.

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:34.170
Let's say you want to
write a C-Sharp grader,

00:36:34.170 --> 00:36:36.690
let's say you want to write
something completely different,

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:38.700
you can use that.

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:42.660
The tools, for instance, we
require you to update the tools

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:45.090
or upload the tools on an
MCP server or something.

00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:46.230
Say you don't want to do that.

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:49.470
Say your tools are in some
format that are not compatible

00:36:49.470 --> 00:36:50.910
with our high-level APIs.

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:53.730
You can use that now because you
have full control over invoking

00:36:53.730 --> 00:36:55.370
of the tools in the
sampling process.

00:36:55.370 --> 00:36:57.860
Alicia Frame: Or I could
even have interactions

00:36:57.860 --> 00:36:58.880
with an environment.

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:02.630
So, execute the rollout,
call real tools

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:05.070
in the real world,
and see what happens.

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:05.880
Omkar More: Correct.

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:08.070
And then, once you do this,
this is the GRPO step

00:37:08.070 --> 00:37:09.180
where you compute
the advantages,

00:37:09.180 --> 00:37:11.910
like basically take the
mean of the samples and see

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:13.170
which ones are better.

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:15.030
And then, use the
forward-backward pass

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:18.480
to go ahead and
compute the gradients.

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:20.730
Behind the scenes,
we have two nodes.

00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:24.810
One is called the training node,
and one is the sampling node.

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:27.720
And in the training node, we
run the forward-backward pass,

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:30.600
compute the gradients, and
then when you call sync,

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:35.460
we actually sync these
LoRAs to the sampling node.

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:38.130
And all of this requires
a lot of telemetry.

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:40.530
It's hard work that
you don't have to do

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:41.760
if you have to do it yourself.

00:37:41.760 --> 00:37:44.700
Let's say you could very much
build it this way, but you have

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:46.350
to deal with nickel issues,
you would have to deal

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:48.180
with vLLM issues, as you said.

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:50.760
So we abstract all
of this out for you.

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:53.580
And once you're done, you can go
ahead and call sync checkpoint

00:37:53.580 --> 00:37:56.490
and save the checkpoint
for training later on.

00:37:56.490 --> 00:37:58.128
And the great
thing about this is,

00:37:58.152 --> 00:37:59.790
this is not outside of Foundry.

00:37:59.790 --> 00:38:01.640
All of this is within Foundry.

00:38:01.640 --> 00:38:04.820
So you will see this
as a run on Foundry.

00:38:04.820 --> 00:38:08.300
You can use the models as
any other model in Foundry.

00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:10.670
You can use it with
hosted agents.

00:38:10.670 --> 00:38:11.810
You can use it with evaluation.

00:38:11.810 --> 00:38:14.670
The entire ecosystem is
right there for you.

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:20.820
So, with that, let me quickly
run this demo, and we'll see.

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:24.220
So, I have this as a --

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:30.450
So, in here, I have
a launcher script

00:38:30.450 --> 00:38:34.470
that actually
