00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:40.200
You'll see that the grand
norm is reducing down.

00:39:40.200 --> 00:39:42.540
So all of this information
is much more fine-grained

00:39:42.540 --> 00:39:44.600
that you get with this setup.

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:46.490
You will also see
the KL divergence.

00:39:46.490 --> 00:39:49.950
You will see the group
composition becoming better

00:39:49.950 --> 00:39:50.700
over time as well.

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:52.230
Alicia Frame: So I can
have a lot more control

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:54.210
over what I can even
see about this run.

00:39:54.210 --> 00:39:56.670
So KL divergence is kind

00:39:56.670 --> 00:39:59.850
of number one investigation
for reward hacking.

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:01.340
Couldn't see that before.

00:40:01.340 --> 00:40:02.120
Now I can.

00:40:02.120 --> 00:40:03.920
Omkar More: Yeah.

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:06.080
And this allows you to
have the full control

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:08.810
of all the telemetry
on your side.

00:40:08.810 --> 00:40:09.830
You can tweak.

00:40:09.830 --> 00:40:12.320
Say your recipe is ongoing,

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:15.140
and you mid-way decide that,
"Hey, I want to change this

00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:16.580
to use a different
sampling strategy."

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:17.480
You can do that, and that's

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:20.240
where the interactive --

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:21.510
Alicia Frame: Or I could
use curriculum learning.

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:21.720
I can do whatever I want.

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:22.950
Omkar More: You can use
curriculum learning to start

00:40:22.950 --> 00:40:25.470
with a very simple task
and then graduate it

00:40:25.470 --> 00:40:26.760
to a larger and larger task.

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:28.490
You can do that, too.

00:40:28.490 --> 00:40:30.680
Say you don't like GRPO,
you want to experiment

00:40:30.680 --> 00:40:34.790
with some other mechanism, PPO
or DPO, and things like that,

00:40:34.790 --> 00:40:37.850
you can change that, and you can
