00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:08.100
I am going to do this demo.

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:11.430
And I actually don't trust
Claude and Copilot CLI

00:42:11.430 --> 00:42:12.660
to do this on the fly,

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:15.300
and because I'm not running
a fine-tuned model,

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:16.500
it's a little slow.

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:18.330
So I ran this ahead of time.

00:42:18.330 --> 00:42:21.060
But basically, you can see
what I did is I said, "Hey,

00:42:21.060 --> 00:42:22.920
I've got a hosted agent here."

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:24.750
I gave it the address
of my hosted agent,

00:42:24.750 --> 00:42:26.550
and I told it what
I wanted it to do.

00:42:26.550 --> 00:42:28.680
This is Omkar's first demo.

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:30.990
I have an agent.

00:42:30.990 --> 00:42:32.820
I want to figure out
how well it does.

00:42:32.820 --> 00:42:35.610
So step one is I need
to make a grader.

00:42:35.610 --> 00:42:38.640
I want it to grade to make sure
it's calling the right tools.

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:40.850
I want it to give
partial credit.

00:42:40.850 --> 00:42:42.620
Please come up with that grader,

00:42:42.620 --> 00:42:45.050
and then report how well
the model performs.

00:42:45.050 --> 00:42:47.750
And I don't have to
learn the API, the SDK.

00:42:47.750 --> 00:42:50.610
I don't have to click
around in that UI.

00:42:50.610 --> 00:42:53.760
All it's going to do is
use the fine-tuning skill.

00:42:53.760 --> 00:42:55.350
It knows how to build graders.

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:57.930
It knows how to call our APIs.

00:42:57.930 --> 00:43:01.080
And great, it's done
the baseline for me.

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:05.220
It came up with the grader, so
it gives full credit if it asks

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:08.040
for clarification or if
it gets the answer right,

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:11.370
gives partial credit if it
gets some of the tool calls.

00:43:11.370 --> 00:43:14.190
And then we go ahead, and
we grade the teacher model.

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:15.810
So this is our 5.4.

00:43:15.810 --> 00:43:18.390
It's a little different because
this is a little different every

00:43:18.390 --> 00:43:24.300
time I run it, but it gives
a mean score of 8.37,

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:27.180
pass rate at 8 of about 78%.

00:43:27.180 --> 00:43:29.190
Teacher model is
doing pretty well.

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:30.030
Good. Okay.

00:43:30.030 --> 00:43:34.560
Step two. I want to create
my fine-tuned model.

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:37.500
So I'm going to go ahead and
kick off a distillation run.

00:43:37.500 --> 00:43:40.800
To do that, all I say is let's
kick off a distillation run.

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:44.130
I do say my goal is I want
it to be cheaper and faster,

00:43:44.130 --> 00:43:45.450
and that's going to help it pick

00:43:45.450 --> 00:43:48.030
which models to
fine-tune for me.

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:51.720
I want it to take my agent
traces from that agent I shared,

00:43:51.720 --> 00:43:54.180
export them, filter
them, de-dup them,

00:43:54.180 --> 00:43:56.970
and kick off a fine-tuning
autopilot run.

00:43:56.970 --> 00:44:00.390
What that's going to do is it's
going to decide based on my data

00:44:00.390 --> 00:44:04.470
and what I've described, which
models to run, which algorithm

00:44:04.470 --> 00:44:07.230
to pick, and run a couple
different experiments

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:09.300
and see how it goes.

00:44:09.300 --> 00:44:12.660
And so, what it's going to do
is I had it submit those jobs,

00:44:12.660 --> 00:44:16.740
and in parallel, I wanted the
same kind of honesty check

00:44:16.740 --> 00:44:18.750
that the base
models, the smaller,

00:44:18.750 --> 00:44:21.930
cheaper base models are not
just great on their own.

00:44:21.930 --> 00:44:24.720
So I ask it to do the same
thing we showed you earlier.

00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:27.120
Let's evaluate the smaller,
cheaper base models,

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.490
and we'll see how we do.

00:44:29.490 --> 00:44:32.940
And so, it ran, it
kicked off the baseline,

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:35.280
and I ran this before the
session, but you can see,

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:38.400
it got the base
model evals first.

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:40.980
Then, it finished training.

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:43.680
And just like before, we
have our leaderboard.

00:44:43.680 --> 00:44:44.730
I had a winner.

00:44:44.730 --> 00:44:47.700
I came up with a
fine-tuned 4.1 mini.

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:51.030
It picked mini and nano because
