00:45:06.330 --> 00:45:08.460
but with totally
natural language.

00:45:08.460 --> 00:45:12.840
And this is part of the GitHub
Copilot for Azure skill,

00:45:12.840 --> 00:45:15.180
or you can download a
separate fine-tuning skill

00:45:15.180 --> 00:45:17.340
if you just want it
as a standalone.

00:45:17.340 --> 00:45:20.070
And this really makes
fine-tuning accessible

00:45:20.070 --> 00:45:21.540
and super easy.

00:45:21.540 --> 00:45:23.460
So anyone can fine-tune.

00:45:23.460 --> 00:45:25.290
And the cool thing
is, what it'll do is,

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:29.040
if these all actually just made
it worse, it'll look at the logs

00:45:29.040 --> 00:45:32.490
and decide what it needs to
do next: generate more data,

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:37.500
try a different experiment, and
iterate until it gets it right.

00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:41.370
And so, just to end, we
want a take-home message.

00:45:41.370 --> 00:45:44.310
One thing we hear a lot --
we're going to dispel two myths.

00:45:44.310 --> 00:45:47.490
The first is that, "I don't
need to fine-tune anymore

00:45:47.490 --> 00:45:50.340
because frontier models
keep getting smarter.

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:51.930
Why would I need to fine-tune?"

00:45:51.930 --> 00:45:54.750
And the real answer is, "Yes,
they're getting smarter,

00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:57.330
but they're also getting bigger,
slower, and more expensive."

00:45:57.330 --> 00:46:01.740
And you saw in the keynote
today, we can fine-tune small,

00:46:01.740 --> 00:46:05.370
cheap models to outperform
frontier-class models.

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:08.850
And the advantage when
you fine-tune is your IP,

00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:11.190
your business knowledge,
your domain, goes

00:46:11.190 --> 00:46:13.230
into your custom model.

00:46:13.230 --> 00:46:16.080
It's not going into that
frontier lab's weights for them

00:46:16.080 --> 00:46:17.580
to make money off of you.

00:46:17.580 --> 00:46:20.340
You keep your IP, you
get a smarter model

00:46:20.340 --> 00:46:22.380
and better performance.

00:46:22.380 --> 00:46:25.590
The other thing I want to touch
on is often people say, "Okay.

00:46:25.590 --> 00:46:26.190
That was cool.

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:27.990
You showed me some cost savings.

00:46:27.990 --> 00:46:30.510
It costs a lot to fine-tune.

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:31.710
You have to pay to train.

00:46:31.710 --> 00:46:33.150
You have to pay to host.

00:46:33.150 --> 00:46:35.100
I don't want to do this."

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:38.310
The real answer is, Omkar already
showed you developer tier

00:46:38.310 --> 00:46:41.820
training, that's spot
capacity, 50% off.

00:46:41.820 --> 00:46:43.890
The median supervised
fine-tuning job

00:46:43.890 --> 00:46:46.290
on our platform costs
about a dollar.

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:48.120
It's not super expensive.

00:46:48.120 --> 00:46:51.300
And we've introduced things
like developer tier hosting,

00:46:51.300 --> 00:46:52.920
where there is no hosting fee.

00:46:52.920 --> 00:46:55.350
So experimentation
is really easy.

00:46:55.350 --> 00:46:58.770
It gives you the bridge to get
to production, and from there,

00:46:58.770 --> 00:47:03.750
as your usage ramps up, you
see substantial cost savings.

